From 64681686b288eefebfe3bc4232a534e6971cc713 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: zirize <zirize@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 13:42:14 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 1/9] Add an agent socket control channel

A Unix socket, one JSON request per line, that lets a program drive the
machine the way a person drives the window: keys and stick, disks, memory
and registers, the screen, save states, and frames.

Frames are the point of it. An agent cannot keep up with an emulator in
real time, so the machine is parked and run a step at a time by whoever
is connected, which makes a session reproducible: the same state and the
same inputs give the same cycle counts and the same screen. run_frames
advances it, run_until_change waits inside the emulator rather than
having a client poll the screen over the socket, and screen_hash follows
whichever page is actually being displayed so text-mode programs are not
watched on a hi-res page that never changes.

The socket also sees what a person does at the window. The frontends log
every keystroke and stick movement to the agent as well, stamped with the
cycle it happened on, so a recorder can capture a live session and play
it back. What is logged for a key is the byte the keyboard will latch,
not the key the frontend named - shift, caps lock, layout and custom maps
are the keyboard's business, and it is asked through a new
keyboard_query_translate rather than guessed at.

Sound can be recorded from the socket too. The dumper was wired to the
SDL frontends only, which left headless - the one place a recording plays
back deterministically - with nothing. The samples the core hands the
frontend are stereo at 44100, the same shape the SDL frontends dump, and
they arrive on emulated time, so a session replayed as fast as the host
allows still writes a file that plays at the right speed.

Three fixes came out of testing it:

  * joy_axis was sending SET_TRIM, a shared centre offset, instead of
    SET_AXIS, so both sticks moved together and the second stick's axes
    folded onto the first one's Y. A one-stick game hid it.
  * Slot 0 dispatches a command to every peripheral in it, and the
    keyboard's event id and the joystick's set-button id are both 1. The
    joystick only checked that the payload was big enough, so a key event
    was read as a button payload and Ctrl-A released a held button.
  * The frontend's joystick path passed Linux's -32767..32767 straight
    through, so every stick movement went to the rail.

An idle session used to hold a core at full clock. The agent loop blocks
in poll() when the machine is paused and paces itself to the Apple II's
frame rate when it is running, which takes it from 500 wake-ups a second
to 5.

Built with -DENABLE_AGENT (default on) and covered by
tests/test_agent_commands.cpp - 34 cases, no game disk needed.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
